    "summary": "Somaliland's President Abdirahman Mohamed Abdullahi Cirro stated in Nairobi that talks with Somalia could resume, but only if previous agreements are fully implemented first.",
    "content": "Somaliland's President Abdirahman Mohamed Abdullahi Cirro said on Saturday that dialogue between Somalia and Somaliland could be restarted, but he made the implementation of earlier agreements a precondition. He made the remarks while addressing diplomats, international agency heads, and development partners in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\nCirro emphasized that Somaliland has repeatedly shown readiness to engage in negotiations. However, he argued that the accords reached in previous rounds have not been fully honored, and that this gap must be addressed before new talks can begin. His statement comes amid ongoing efforts to resolve the long-standing political status of Somaliland.\n\nThe two sides have held several rounds of talks in recent years, facilitated by international mediators, but progress has been slow. The most recent discussions were stalled over differences on the agenda and the implementation of prior commitments. The President's condition appears to set a clear benchmark for the next step.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant because stability in the Horn of Africa directly affects remittances, investment, and family ties. Many in the diaspora have hoped for a peaceful resolution that would allow for greater economic integration and easier movement across borders. The renewed insistence on implementing prior agreements may signal a more structured approach to negotiations, though no date for new talks has been announced.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/08/cirro-oo-shardi-ku-xiray-dib-u-bilaabista-wada-hadallada-soomaaliya-iyo-somaliland/",
